WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.092 --> 00:00:03.300
(upbeat music)

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.583
- So, this is the Rabbit R1.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:11.160
And it's another AI in a box,
and it's a sign of the times.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:11.993
So this thing here,

00:00:11.993 --> 00:00:15.450
it has a lot in common
with the Humane Ai Pin,

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.650
for better or for worse.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.950
But it's also supposed to
have two specific things

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.473
that are supposed to
set it apart from that.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.290
Supposed to, anyway.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.675
And we have to talk about it.

00:00:26.675 --> 00:00:29.370
(letters clicking)
(tense music)

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.890
So, stop me if this sounds
familiar at any point,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.600
but check this out.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.410
This is a virtual assistant in a box.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:38.610
But it's a different box.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:39.900
This one is not wearable.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.880
It's a thing you have
to carry around with you

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.440
in your pocket, like a smartphone.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.500
I'd say it's about the size
of a stack of Post-its, maybe.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.990
But it's this lightweight plastic cube,

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.460
designed by Teenage Engineering.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.190
And boy, do people love some
Teenage Engineering right now.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.180
And it's super...

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.090
I mean, you can tell exactly
what it is from a mile away.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.650
This thing is very recognizable.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.480
The thing has one button
on the right side.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.210
That's the one you press and
hold to ask it questions.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:04.560
And then instead of a projector,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.020
it actually does have
a built-in screen here.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.430
And instead of built-in cellular,

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.290
it has a SIM card tray right alongside

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.450
that USB Type-C port for charging.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.010
I'd say it does seem to send basically

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.380
every single request to
the Cloud, but I will say,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.630
it does feel significantly quicker

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.660
to answer questions
than the Humane Ai Pin.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.380
Now, that is a very,
very low bar to clear,

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.800
but it's consistent, it's
consistently quicker.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.050
How far away is the moon?

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:35.400
- [AI Voice] The average distance

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.540
between Earth and the Moon

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.890
is approximately 384,400 kilometers.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.600
- You can connect
headphones via Bluetooth,

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.510
or turn the built-in speaker
all the way up, like I have.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.850
But it also shows you
the text of your answer

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.160
on the screen, right underneath
this bouncing rabbit,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:51.660
and the time in the battery.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.210
Matter of fact, it basically always shows

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.430
this bouncing rabbit all the time.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.990
It's how you know it's awake.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.150
You hit that button
once to put it to sleep.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.370
Or it would do that automatically
after a few seconds.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.080
Then hit the button again to wake it up.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.510
Boom, just the rabbit
waiting for you there.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.950
And then to get to settings,

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.570
there's no button to press for that.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.310
There's no gesture on
the screen or anything.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.370
You just shake it like an Etch A Sketch.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:15.300
And as long as you do that,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.220
then from there you can scroll up and down

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.890
with this scroll wheel,
and select with the button.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.260
That's how you get around the UI.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.460
So, the other two things it has

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.520
are the scroll wheel
and a swiveling camera.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.800
So the scroll wheel for
navigating around the UI

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.470
instead of the screen is...

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.910
I'll get to why it's odd in a second.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.510
But then of course, the AI
assistant is multimodal,

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.240
and so you can use vision
to answer questions

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.560
about what it sees.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.760
So we've seen this before.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.250
With this one, you
double-tap to swivel it open,

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.470
and then press and hold to ask.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.520
I don't know. What type of plant is this?

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.100
- [AI Voice] Taking a look now.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.580
The plant in the image appears
to be a Monstera deliciosa,

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.920
also known as a Swiss cheese plant.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.040
This is a type of tropical
climbing evergreen plant

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.760
native to southern Mexico
and parts of Central America.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.950
The large glossy green
leaves with distinctive-

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.180
- As a reviewer testing this thing,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.400
I just feel like that DJ
Khaled clip after a while,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.540
where I'm just pointing at stuff,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.790
like, "Okay, what is this?

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.123
And what is this?"

00:03:16.123 --> 00:03:16.956
- And what is this?

00:03:16.956 --> 00:03:18.524
- [Speaker] Those are
pickled banana peppers.

00:03:18.524 --> 00:03:19.519
- [DJ Khaled] And what is this?

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:21.000
- [Speaker] Berries and seeds.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.950
- [DJ Khaled] And perhaps what is this?

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:23.783
- [Speaker] Water.

00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:25.950
- But I've also, I've just
pointed it at my computer screen

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.210
with a really long email on it,

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.680
and asked it for a summary,

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.020
and it just reads an instantly
and gives me a summary of it.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.730
It's done that with articles too.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.110
I think that's pretty cool.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.640
But yeah, it's just a...

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.110
It's an AI in a box.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.547
It's best at just answering questions.

00:03:40.547 --> 00:03:42.420
(text clicking)
(soft music)

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.480
So yeah, this thing is
also bad at a lot of stuff.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.970
And this list is gonna
sound pretty familiar.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:51.750
Somehow, the battery life is
just as bad as the Humane Pin.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.450
It has a thousand million
power battery inside

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.860
and it's brutally bad.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:56.970
Like it's already bad enough

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.860
when you're carrying around
a whole nother device

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.750
alongside your smartphone,

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.690
but when this one can sit in
front of you doing nothing,

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.240
and the battery just visibly is draining

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.190
and dies in like four hours...

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.320
Then you have to charge
it multiple times per day,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.420
and it's still dead when
you wake up in the morning.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:13.950
Like, it's just exhausting.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.370
Also, it took 45 minutes
to charge this tiny battery

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.540
from zero to a hundred.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.340
And it's also just straight up missing

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.100
a ton of what I would
consider just basic features.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.560
Like it can't set
alarms, can't set timers,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.200
it can't record videos,
can't record photos,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.110
can't send emails, there's
no calendar built in.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:33.330
There's just a lot of
things that I would want

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.890
an assistant to do...

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:35.723
Not here.

00:04:35.723 --> 00:04:37.350
And of course, being an AI assistant,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.960
it also does still hallucinate,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.480
and confidently answer questions wrong.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.370
Like my baseline of asking a question

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:45.750
that I know the answer to,

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:49.230
and then getting the wrong
answer happens all the time.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:50.790
Which is one of the
downfalls of this category.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.240
So this device was designed
by Teenage Engineering.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.860
And they're really leaning into that.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:56.693
Like that's why...

00:04:56.693 --> 00:05:00.150
I mean, it's this bright
orange, it is really quirky,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.170
friendly looking device, I
would say, intentionally.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.930
But yeah, they really love
their analog controls.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:10.080
And this scroll wheel
here doesn't surprise me,

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.680
but it's actually really
frustrating to use.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.140
First of all, it protrudes
out the back a little bit,

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:16.973
as you can see.

00:05:16.973 --> 00:05:18.750
Which looks kinda cool.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.670
But that means if it was really sensitive,

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.980
that it would actually scroll
if you just put it down

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.510
on a table or something.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.970
So they've dialed down the
sensitivity of the scroll wheel,

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.460
so it's actually super slow.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.530
Like there's a surprising
amount of scroll motion

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.530
required to go down one
line in the settings.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.870
And then there's no haptic
feedback here as you're scrolling

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.460
to help you feel it out.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.380
So fine, okay, you can get used to that.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.150
You use the button to select...

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:43.530
But then you've also noticed,

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:46.080
there's no back button
anywhere on this device.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.610
So to go back up a level,

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.760
you have to scroll all
the way back up to the top

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.100
every single time.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.510
That also gets annoying.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.450
And then to change brightness or volume,

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:58.680
you actually need two hands.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.447
So you just go into brightness to select,

00:06:00.447 --> 00:06:03.060
and you hold the button with one hand,

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.250
and then scroll the scroll wheel

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.830
with the other hand to adjust brightness.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:10.380
Which, yeah, you can't
do it with one hand.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.553
It works, so you can learn
it, and you can say, you know,

00:06:13.553 --> 00:06:15.360
it's this quirky UI it's got going on.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.330
But I feel like a lot of
these problems would be solved

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.163
if this was a touch screen.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.360
So what if I told you
this is a touch screen,

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.340
and they just don't really let you use it

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:27.360
for almost anything?

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.550
Like moving through these
menus would be easier

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.740
if I could just tap
what I wanted to, right?

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.530
Going back after a long
scroll would be easier

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.810
if I could just flip scroll
back to the top of a list,

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:37.950
and hit back.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:38.783
But you can't.

00:06:38.783 --> 00:06:40.500
The only thing you can
use the touchscreen for

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.560
is basically typing on the
keyboard in terminal mode.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:47.040
So with terminal enabled,
you can turn it sideways,

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.950
a keyboard pops up, and
you can type your questions

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:51.180
and get text answers.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.013
Which is great.

00:06:52.013 --> 00:06:54.660
And you can move between
letters with the scroll wheel.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.100
It's kind of a neat feature, I guess.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:00.540
But why can't we use the touch
screen for anything else?

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.730
Is it just them trying
not to look too much

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:04.770
like a smartphone?

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:05.603
Maybe.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:07.764
Probably.

00:07:07.764 --> 00:07:09.720
(text clicking)
(tense music)

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.000
So, okay, what's the point?

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:12.960
Why does this thing exist?

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.340
If it's so similar to the other one

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.490
that was also so bad,
what are we doing here?

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:18.810
And really, there's two things

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:20.970
that I think they're
hoping will be the things

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:23.910
that set it apart from the other one.

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.640
That should, in the words of
their co-founder, Jesse...

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.280
- But I don't think MKBHD will say

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.263
this is the worst device
he ever reviewed so far.

00:07:31.263 --> 00:07:32.096
(people laughing)

00:07:32.096 --> 00:07:33.870
- Which, low bar, but okay.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.350
Those two things are the price tag,

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:38.550
and the large action model.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:42.120
So the Humane Pin was so
easy for everyone to dunk on

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.280
because it costs as much as a phone.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.810
It was $700 with a $24
a month subscription

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:50.550
to not turn into a brick.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:51.570
It's just insane.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:56.570
So, this one right here costs
$200 and no subscription.

00:07:57.090 --> 00:08:00.240
So, you know, okay, that
hits a little different.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.840
But also, you can tell it's $200.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.940
First of all, you will
need a separate SIM card

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.380
to get it to work on cellular.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:09.930
So while there is no
subscription fee for Rabbit

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.400
to keep the device working,

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.050
that is still a fee you're
gonna pay every month

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.060
to get data outside of wifi.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.010
But then, okay, the unboxing experience

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:18.510
is extremely minimal.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:20.460
It comes in a single cardboard box

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.620
with a cassette tape
looking plastic container,

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.030
that doubles as a stand,

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:25.590
but there's literally nothing else.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:29.100
No charging brick, no
USB-C cable, no stickers,

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.800
no paper instruction
manual, nothing at all.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.980
And then the R1 itself is made of plastic.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.240
Not to say that it's not built well.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.740
You know, there's no flexing or creaking

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:38.573
or anything like that.

00:08:38.573 --> 00:08:40.260
But it's definitely plastic.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.570
The camera, very basic.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:44.640
The speaker, very cheap.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.770
You know, it's a low end
MediaTek chip inside.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.410
The same one that's in
the Moto G8 Power Lite,

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.150
which is like $150 phone.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.130
Hardly any battery, as we learned.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:56.100
And no fancy fast charging
or wireless charging.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.400
And it comes in one color,
really bright orange.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.060
Like this ridiculously
saturated bright orange.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:05.160
I'm not even joking,
the color you're seeing

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.770
in this YouTube video on your screen,

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:09.420
it's gonna be the best I can do

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:10.710
with my own color correction.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.680
But it's legitimately hard to photograph.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.350
Like the phone camera doesn't really

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:17.790
turn saturation up high enough

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.800
to really represent it
accurately in real life.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.030
It's the brightest orange thing

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:23.100
you've ever seen in your
life, I guarantee it.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:25.140
Now, if you want something
a little more low key

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:26.460
than the neon orange,

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.950
channel sponsor dbrand actually
does have you covered here.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.020
I've got this black camo looking one here,

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:32.940
and now the orange bits that show through

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.730
are kind of like more accent
pieces, which is pretty dope.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.860
But fun fact, dbrand also
collaborated with Rabbit

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:38.970
ahead of the release to make sure

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:40.500
screen protectors for this thing

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.330
were also available on launch day.

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:43.410
So, that includes mine.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:44.340
If you wanna check it out,

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:47.040
screen protectors or skins for the R1,

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.090
you can hit up the link below.

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:49.380
But yes, speaking of the screen,

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:54.270
this is a 2.9 inch diagonal TFT screen,

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:55.800
with no auto brightness.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.581
So, yeah, I think you get the idea.

00:09:58.581 --> 00:10:00.720
(letters clicking)
(soft music)

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:04.710
So you know how the
Humane Ai Pin had no apps?

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:06.900
Well, this thing also has no apps.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:08.220
But the other thing that they're hoping

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.290
will separate it from the other stuff,

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.930
is what they're calling
a large action model.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:16.170
So you know, large language
models are just the words,

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:17.220
the language that we use.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.740
So it's an AI that can
take our natural language,

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:23.040
and process it, and turn
it into words in return.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:24.840
And that's how we interact with it.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.220
So a large action model is
supposed to take our words,

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:32.790
and then process it, and
turn it into actions.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:33.623
That's the theory.

00:10:33.623 --> 00:10:37.740
So it's basically going to
be able to use apps for you

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:38.760
like a human would,

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:39.960
based on what you tell it to do.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:43.080
Now, importantly, this
isn't the same as an API,

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.510
where you know, a company
might work with the others

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.023
to have some sort of a
plug into their services

00:10:48.023 --> 00:10:49.890
so that they work together.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.200
Because an API can be a
little too restrictive,

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.960
or not give all the features,
or just not quite...

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.100
I mean, there's lots of good reasons

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:58.380
why they don't wanna rely on an API.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:00.090
So what this is supposed to be doing,

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:04.470
is just going in and using
the app, just like a human,

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.420
essentially with like
a mouse and a keyboard.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.610
Think of it as like a virtual agent.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.530
Honestly, I think it's a really cool idea.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.800
I think it's a really, really cool idea.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.690
You know, large language
models have been trained,

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:16.860
with all their data,

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:19.530
to talk back to us just like humans would,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.360
and some are very convincing.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.910
So a large action model, in theory,

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:27.150
should be able to use
these apps and services

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.100
just like a human would.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:33.450
From Spotify to Twitter,
to your banking app,

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.740
to whatever else.

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:39.180
It's already good enough at
recognizing major UI elements

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:42.810
like a play button or a buy
button and things like that.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:45.753
So with enough training
data, could get really good.

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:50.853
With enough training data,
could get really good.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:52.710
But the thing is,

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:55.980
they don't have a ton
of training data yet.

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:00.980
So as of right now, they have
made four apps available.

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:02.123
Four.

00:12:02.123 --> 00:12:04.110
They have this online portal
called the Rabbit Hole,

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:07.500
where you can log into
and enable all of them.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:12.000
Spotify, Uber, DoorDash and Midjourney.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.880
So the Rabbit can talk to these four apps,

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.700
and has a UI on the screen for doing so

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:19.560
in the exact way that we're theorizing.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:21.480
So it'll play Spotify songs.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.220
Like I can ask it for a song,

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:24.930
and it'll try to play the right one,

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:28.050
and it'll show you any info
you need on the touch screen

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.450
to confirm or refine the
actions you're taking.

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.760
But even now, they kinda work.

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.410
Like, I've already had issues with it

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:35.970
playing the wrong song.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:37.830
I've also seen other
people already have issues

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:39.600
with the DoorDash app
getting things wrong,

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:41.280
which is even more frustrating.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:43.200
I can't imagine how
frustrating the Uber app

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:44.400
messing up would be.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.710
It definitely seems like it
needs more training data.

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:48.990
And that's just for
these four starting apps.

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.030
Now, Rabbit says they have

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:54.630
800 different apps trained already,

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.150
but they just haven't
built a UI for it yet,

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.610
so it's not available on the Rabbit yet.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.580
And so that's still in the works.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:03.990
Now they've also theoretically

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:06.660
started working on something
called a generative UI,

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:08.370
where essentially it can recognize

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:10.020
what type of app it is,

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:13.650
and then build a UI for it
so Rabbit doesn't have to...

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:15.780
But that's also still theoretical,

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.300
and also on the roadmap
and still in the works.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:20.010
And then if there's another app or service

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.200
that you want your
Rabbit to be able to do,

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:23.640
like something for work,

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:26.520
or something super niche
that you've invented,

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:29.640
they've talked about
something called "Teach Mode".

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.120
Which is basically having
the Rabbit watch you

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.060
do what you want it to do
on your mouse and keyboard,

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.100
and then it learns from what you did,

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:41.670
and can then repeat your
actions later, which is sick.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.620
Unfortunately, you can probably already

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:44.453
see where this is going,

00:13:44.453 --> 00:13:47.820
Teach Mode is also not
available as of right now.

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:49.050
This is something they're still working on

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:49.920
vetting and finishing.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.310
And so that's just later
this year, also in the works.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.170
So as of right now,

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:59.520
this device has none of that stuff,

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:01.740
just the four kind of half working apps

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.513
that we went over at the beginning.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.528
What are we doing here?

00:14:06.528 --> 00:14:08.760
(letters clicking)
(soft music)

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:11.850
Okay, I'm gonna try not
to turn this into a rant,

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.970
but I feel like we need
to acknowledge at least,

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.580
that a lot of these tech companies

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:21.060
are developing tech kinda backwards.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:24.360
Like they're delivering
such unfinished products,

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:27.570
that it actually makes them
nearly impossible to review.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.520
Like, it feels like it used to just be,

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:32.490
make the thing, and then put it on sale.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.920
Now it's like put it on sale,

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:36.900
and then deliver the half-baked thing,

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:38.430
and then iterate and make it better,

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:40.710
and hopefully with enough
updates, then it's ready,

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:41.940
and it's what we promised

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:43.920
way back when we first started selling it.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.200
And then this whole period
in the middle is a mess.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:48.600
And it's across all kinds
of product categories too.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:49.830
We've seen this with gaming.

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:53.917
Like huge studios are delivering
half-baked games saying,

00:14:53.917 --> 00:14:55.560
"Oh, you know, it's an alpha version.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.000
There will be updates."

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.670
But meanwhile, it's a full price AAA game

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:01.560
that's just gotten an unacceptable number

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:03.000
of bugs and issues.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.190
It's also happening with cars.

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:06.720
And vehicles getting announced,

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:09.570
and then delivering with
like a half-finished state,

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.880
where you just don't get
a lot of the features

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:12.713
that you paid for.

00:15:12.713 --> 00:15:14.610
And they're eventually coming soon,

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:15.630
with a software update.

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:16.710
You know, smartphones, obviously,

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:18.480
we've been seeing this for years,

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.760
but it does seem like now more than ever,

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:23.070
there's at least one feature,

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:25.380
one major feature of
every smartphone launch

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:26.550
that gets announced,

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:28.980
but that's not coming
until later in the year.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:32.700
And now these AI based
products are at like the apex

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:34.890
of this horrible trend where,

00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:36.990
the thing that you get at the beginning

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:39.810
is like borderline nonfunctional

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:42.330
compared to all the promises
and all the features

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:45.390
and all the things that it's
supposed to maybe someday be.

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:47.610
But you still pay full
price at the beginning,

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:48.840
which is what makes it so crazy.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.160
And that sucks not only obviously

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.530
for the people buying it at
full price at the beginning,

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:55.380
but also for reviewing
these things in general.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:58.800
Like, how do you assess a product

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:03.120
where the version of what's
promised in like three years,

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.760
what it could be is amazing,

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:09.900
but the version that's being
delivered now is dog water.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.660
Like, how do you connect those dots?

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:13.950
Do you even connect those dots?

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:16.290
Are you supposed to give them
the benefit of the doubt?

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:17.580
I don't know. What are we even doing here?

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:18.720
I mean, I guess on one hand,

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:21.420
it is good that tech products,

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:23.970
these things can get better
over time and improve,

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:26.220
and the thing you buy
can be better tomorrow.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:27.053
That's all great.

00:16:27.053 --> 00:16:28.440
That didn't used to be true,

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:29.610
and that's beautiful and everything.

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:31.350
But on the other hand,

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:33.360
it's just, the other side of that coin,

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:37.350
is some very unfinished
products being delivered,

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:40.050
and I just feel like it's gonna get worse

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:41.164
before it gets better.

00:16:41.164 --> 00:16:43.740
(letters clicking)
(tense music)

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:45.450
So, let me just say,

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:48.030
I am personally very excited

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:53.030
by the idea of a super
personalized AI assistant

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:56.730
that can do everything that
a human assistant could.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:58.230
That's my dream. I want that to happen.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:00.810
And I'm interested that
these different companies

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:03.450
are approaching it from different angles.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.820
And hopefully, we'll get there eventually.

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:07.800
But this is also so clearly going to take

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:11.940
a lot of time and effort
and technical development,

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:13.560
and also a lot of data.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:16.200
Like, I've said this already
in the Humane review,

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:19.530
I'm pretty sure, which is a
good assistant needs to know

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:22.470
everything about you, whether
it's human or virtual.

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:24.240
Like it needs to know your preferences,

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:25.440
and your location,

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:27.090
and what you're doing,
and what you like to do.

00:17:27.090 --> 00:17:28.620
And every single little thing.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:29.640
It's so much data.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:30.920
So as for the Rabbit...

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:35.310
I think that's why they focused
on getting the price so low.

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:36.840
Like, this stuff is all a tough sell,

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:37.950
because you're taking a gamble

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:39.660
on what the product could be someday.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:44.660
But for the Pin, you have to
drop $700, and $24 every month,

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:48.180
on the chance that it
might someday get there.

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:49.680
Like that's a lot of money.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:52.770
But for this cute little thing, 200 bucks.

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:55.110
Feels like a much easier
investment to make.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:56.730
A much easier pill to swallow,

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:58.260
to just take a gamble on it.

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:00.440
And on the off chance it
turns out to be awesome

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:02.940
in a couple of years, it'll
feel like it's worth it.

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:05.730
You know, on the chance
that some of those 800 apps

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:07.500
are super useful.

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:10.050
Or you know, even though you
already have a smartphone,

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:12.180
maybe the Teach Mode lets it do something

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:13.230
with a single button press

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:15.210
that you could never dream of before,

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:16.650
then it could be totally worth it.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:18.450
The whole situation reminds me kind of...

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.160
If you look back at the beginning

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:23.190
of when Tesla started shipping cars

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:26.160
with all the autopilot stuff.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:27.060
They were selling cars,

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:28.920
and they got popular for other reasons.

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:30.660
It was a good electric
car with good range,

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:32.100
and a good charging network.

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:34.710
And then people started
sort of beta testing

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:36.150
this autopilot stuff,

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:38.280
and Tesla starts collecting

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:41.160
millions of miles and hours of data,

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:42.810
from how it works in the real world.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:45.900
And so all of that data
helped their systems learn,

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:47.580
and get the head start that they need,

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:49.710
on trying to make the
best autopilot software.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.210
So the Rabbit, I'm sure,

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:52.980
would like to use their price advantage,

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:54.090
and get as many of them out

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:55.770
into the real world as possible,

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:56.700
into people's hands,

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:57.840
so they can start beta testing,

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.910
and having it trained and getting good

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:01.350
at a variety of tasks.

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:03.210
But the Rabbit's problem is,

00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:05.970
it doesn't have like the other reason

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:08.880
why people would get it anyway today.

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:11.070
So it's like a kind of
chicken and egg problem.

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:15.090
So if you want my advice,
you've heard it before.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:19.710
Buy the product based on what it is today,

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.860
and not what it's promised
to be in the future.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:26.100
And with this category, that's
like the hardest thing to do,

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:27.270
the hardest thing to keep in mind.

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:29.370
'Cause the promise is so big.

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:31.680
But that's still my advice.

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:33.510
And I guess we still also have yet to see

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:37.380
what big companies like Google and Apple

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.870
are gonna try to do in this
space, probably this year.

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:42.963
So, with all of that, we'll see.

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:45.930
Thanks for watching.

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:47.310
Catch you in the next one.

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:48.209
Peace.

00:19:48.209 --> 00:19:51.292
(mellow music fades)

